# Session-Based Implementation: Processor Replacement Patterns

## Purpose
Guide the replacement of mock `@RestController` implementations with real JPA-backed business logic within individual sessions while maintaining test compatibility and code quality standards.

## Session Pre-Implementation Checklist

Before writing any implementation code in the current session:
- [ ] Session plan reviewed (`plan/sessions/session-N-phase-M.md`)
- [ ] `@WebMvcTest` is written and failing
- [ ] Flyway migration created and applied
- [ ] `@Entity` defined (layered architecture — never returned from controllers)
- [ ] `JpaRepository` interface designed
- [ ] `@Service` layer structure planned
- [ ] Previous session dependencies confirmed

## Code Style Guidelines

Follow `rules/300-java-spring-style.mdc` strictly:

### Constructor Injection
```java
// Good
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PostService {
    private final PostRepository postRepository;
}

// Bad
@Service
public class PostService {
    @Autowired
    private PostRepository postRepository;
}
```

### Javadoc
Use Javadoc for all public service and repository methods:
```java
/**
 * Creates a new blog post.
 *
 * @param request the post creation payload
 * @param authorId the authenticated author's identifier
 * @return the persisted post with generated id and timestamps
 * @throws DataIntegrityViolationException if the underlying save fails
 */
@Transactional
public Post createPost(CreatePostRequest request, UUID authorId) {
    // ...
}
```

### Method Length
Keep methods focused and short:
- Prefer under ~40 lines per method
- Single responsibility principle
- Extract complex logic into private helper methods

### Naming
- `camelCase` for variables and methods
- `PascalCase` for classes
- `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants
- Descriptive names, avoid abbreviations

## Session Processor Replacement Pattern

### Step 1: Review Session Plan
Review `plan/sessions/session-N-phase-M.md` to understand:
- Current session objectives
- Endpoint to be implemented
- Dependencies from previous sessions
- Expected deliverables

### Step 2: Identify Mock Code
Locate the mock implementation from Session 1 (Phase 0):
```java
@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<PostResponse> createPost(@Valid @RequestBody CreatePostRequest request) {
    // MOCK IMPLEMENTATION — Phase 0 skeleton
    PostResponse mock = new PostResponse(
        UUID.randomUUID(), request.title(), request.content(), request.authorId(), Instant.now());
    return ResponseEntity.ok(mock);
}
```

### Step 3: Add Service Layer Dependency
Inject the service via the constructor:
```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/posts")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PostController {

    private final PostService postService;
}
```

### Step 4: Delegate to Service, Keep Controller Thin
The controller's job is HTTP translation only:
```java
@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<PostResponse> createPost(
        @Valid @RequestBody CreatePostRequest request,
        @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails currentUser) {

    UUID authorId = UUID.fromString(currentUser.getUsername());
    Post created = postService.createPost(request, authorId);
    return ResponseEntity.ok(PostResponse.from(created));
}
```

### Step 5: Implement Business Logic in the Service
```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class PostService {

    private final PostRepository postRepository;

    @Transactional
    public Post createPost(CreatePostRequest request, UUID authorId) {
        if (request.title().length() < 5) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Title must be at least 5 characters");
        }

        Post post = new Post();
        post.setTitle(request.title());
        post.setContent(request.content());
        post.setAuthorId(authorId);

        Post saved = postRepository.save(post);
        log.info("Created post {} for author {}", saved.getId(), authorId);
        return saved;
    }
}
```

### Complete Implementation

```java
// web/PostController.java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/posts")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PostController {

    private final PostService postService;

    /**
     * Creates a new blog post for the authenticated user.
     */
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<PostResponse> createPost(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreatePostRequest request,
            @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails currentUser) {
        UUID authorId = UUID.fromString(currentUser.getUsername());
        Post created = postService.createPost(request, authorId);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(PostResponse.from(created));
    }
}
```

## Error Handling Patterns

### Input Validation
Use `jakarta.validation` annotations directly on the DTO record:
```java
public record CreatePostRequest(
    @NotBlank @Size(min = 1, max = 200) String title,
    @NotBlank @Size(min = 1, max = 10000) String content,
    @NotNull UUID authorId
) {}
```

### Business Logic Errors
Handle in the service layer with typed exceptions:
```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PostService {

    public Post createPost(CreatePostRequest request, UUID authorId) {
        if (request.title().length() < 5) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Title must be at least 5 characters");
        }
        try {
            return postRepository.save(toEntity(request, authorId));
        } catch (DataIntegrityViolationException e) {
            log.error("Repository error creating post", e);
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```

### Exception Mapping to HTTP Status
Map domain errors to HTTP status codes via `@RestControllerAdvice` (see `rules/300-java-spring-style.mdc`), not with try/catch in every controller method:
```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleBadRequest(IllegalArgumentException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(EntityNotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(EntityNotFoundException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(AccessDeniedException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleForbidden(AccessDeniedException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN, ex.getMessage());
    }
}
```

## Logging Patterns

### Use SLF4J via Lombok's `@Slf4j`
```java
@Service
@Slf4j
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PostService {
    // log is generated automatically by @Slf4j
}
```

### Structured Context
```java
// Good: structured with context via SLF4J parameterized logging
log.info("Post created postId={} authorId={} titleLength={}",
    post.getId(), post.getAuthorId(), post.getTitle().length());

// Bad: string concatenation, no structure
log.info("Created post " + post.getId());
```

### Log Levels
- `DEBUG`: Detailed information for debugging
- `INFO`: General informational messages
- `WARN`: Warning messages for recoverable issues
- `ERROR`: Error messages for failures

### What to Log
```java
// Log significant operations
log.info("Creating post for author {}", authorId);

// Log errors with the exception for stack trace capture
log.error("Failed to create post", e);

// Log performance issues
log.warn("Slow query detected: {}ms", durationMs);

// Never log sensitive data
log.info("User logged in: {}", userId);       // Good
log.info("User password: {}", password);      // BAD — NEVER LOG SECRETS
```

## Transaction Patterns

### Single Operation
```java
@Transactional
public Post createPost(CreatePostRequest request, UUID authorId) {
    return postRepository.save(toEntity(request, authorId));
}
```

### Multiple Operations (Same Transaction)
```java
@Transactional
public Post createPostWithTags(CreatePostRequest request, UUID authorId, List<String> tags) {
    Post post = postRepository.save(toEntity(request, authorId));
    tags.forEach(tag -> tagRepository.save(new PostTag(post.getId(), tag)));
    return post;
}
```

`@Transactional` on a `@Service` method wraps the whole method body in a single database transaction — if any repository call throws, everything rolls back. Never place `@Transactional` on a `@RestController`.

## Performance Considerations

### Avoid N+1 Queries
```java
// Bad: N+1 query problem
List<Post> posts = postRepository.findAll();
posts.forEach(p -> p.getAuthor().getName()); // triggers a query per post

// Good: fetch join in the query method
@Query("SELECT p FROM Post p JOIN FETCH p.author")
List<Post> findAllWithAuthor();
```

### Use Pagination
```java
public Page<Post> getPosts(Pageable pageable) {
    return postRepository.findAll(pageable);
}
```

`JpaRepository` already extends `PagingAndSortingRepository` — `findAll(Pageable)` is available without extra code.

### Cache Expensive Operations
```java
@Cacheable("postStatistics")
public PostStatistics getPostStatistics(UUID postId) {
    // Expensive aggregation query
    return computeStatistics(postId);
}
```
(Requires `@EnableCaching` on a `@Configuration` class and a cache provider such as Caffeine.)

## Session Testing Implementation

After replacing the mock in the current session:

### Run the Test
```bash
./mvnw test -Dtest=PostControllerTest#createPost_returnsCreatedPost
```

### Verify the Test Passes
Tests should now pass with the real implementation:
- Actual UUIDs generated by `GenerationType.UUID`
- Real timestamps from `@CreationTimestamp`
- Data persisted correctly in Postgres

### Add Edge Case Tests
```java
@Test
void createPost_titleTooLong_returns400() { }

@Test
void createPost_duplicateTitle_isAllowed() { } // document the business decision either way
```

## Session Code Review Checklist

Before marking session implementation complete:
- [ ] Session plan objectives met
- [ ] Constructor injection used everywhere (`@RequiredArgsConstructor`)
- [ ] Javadoc on all public service/repository methods
- [ ] Error handling implemented via `@RestControllerAdvice`
- [ ] Logging added for significant operations
- [ ] No hardcoded values
- [ ] No secrets in code
- [ ] Proper `@Transactional` boundaries
- [ ] Performance considerations addressed (pagination, fetch joins)
- [ ] Tests passing for current endpoint
- [ ] Code formatted (`./mvnw spotless:apply`)
- [ ] No `TODO` comments without a tracked ticket
- [ ] Session summary documents implementation

## Session Implementation Anti-Patterns

Do NOT:
- Skip session plan review
- Put business logic in `@RestController` methods
- Access `JpaRepository` directly from a controller
- Return `@Entity` objects to the API layer
- Use bare `catch (Exception e) {}` without re-throwing or mapping
- Log sensitive information (passwords, tokens, full request bodies with PII)
- Hardcode configuration values (use `application.yml` + `@ConfigurationProperties`)
- Skip error handling
- Forget Javadoc on public methods
- Use static/global mutable state
- Implement features not required by the current session
- Implement endpoints from other sessions
- Skip session documentation
